### What is the difference between liveness and readiness?

The key is distinguishing two questions. A liveness endpoint answers "is the process healthy, or is it stuck and should be killed and restarted?" A readiness endpoint answers "can this instance accept traffic right now, or is it still warming up or temporarily overloaded?" In Spring Boot, Actuator exposes `/actuator/health/liveness` and `/actuator/health/readiness`.

```java
package com.bank.health;

import org.springframework.boot.actuate.health.Health;
import org.springframework.boot.actuate.health.HealthIndicator;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import javax.sql.DataSource;
import java.sql.Connection;

@Component
public class DatabaseReadinessIndicator implements HealthIndicator {

    private final DataSource dataSource;

    public DatabaseReadinessIndicator(DataSource dataSource) {
        this.dataSource = dataSource;
    }

    @Override
    public Health health() {
        try (Connection conn = dataSource.getConnection()) {
            if (conn.isValid(2)) {
                return Health.up()
                    .withDetail("database", "connected")
                    .build();
            }
        } catch (Exception e) {
            // Database unreachable: not ready for traffic
        }
        return Health.down()
            .withDetail("database", "unreachable")
            .build();
    }
}
```

```yaml
# application.yml
management:
  endpoint:
    health:
      probes:
        enabled: true
      group:
        readiness:
          include: db, diskSpace, redis
        liveness:
          include: livenessState
  endpoints:
    web:
      exposure:
        include: health, info, prometheus
```

```text
GET /actuator/health/liveness  -> {"status": "UP"}     (process alive)
GET /actuator/health/readiness -> {"status": "DOWN"}   (DB pool broken)
```

### How do heartbeats work with a service registry?

After registering with a service registry like Eureka, the instance sends a periodic renew signal. If the registry misses several in a row, it marks the instance dead and evicts it. The important nuance is the staleness gap: because detection depends on missed heartbeats, there is always a small delay between an instance dying and the registry noticing.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import org.springframework.cloud.netflix.eureka.EnableEurekaClient;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;

@Configuration
@EnableEurekaClient
public class EurekaConfig {
    // Instance registers on startup and sends heartbeat every 30s
    // Registry evicts after 90s of missed heartbeats
}
```

```yaml
# application.yml - Eureka client
eureka:
  instance:
    lease-renewal-interval-in-seconds: 30
    lease-expiration-duration-in-seconds: 90
  client:
    service-url:
      defaultZone: http://eureka-server:8761/eureka/
```

```text
Heartbeat timeline:
  t=0s:   instance registers with Eureka
  t=30s:  heartbeat sent -> registry marks instance alive
  t=60s:  heartbeat sent -> alive
  t=90s:  instance crashes, no heartbeat sent
  t=120s: missed heartbeat #1
  t=150s: missed heartbeat #2
  t=180s: eviction -> registry removes instance from list
  Staleness gap: up to 90 seconds where a dead instance is still listed
```

### How do Kubernetes probes operationalize health endpoints?

Kubernetes probes poll the health endpoints and take automatic action. The liveness probe restarts a stuck container. The readiness probe controls whether the pod receives traffic without killing it. The startup probe holds off liveness until the app has had time to boot, preventing a slow JVM from being killed in a restart loop.

```yaml
# Kubernetes deployment with all three probes
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: account-service
spec:
  template:
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: account-service
          image: bank/account-service:1.0
          ports:
            - containerPort: 8080
          startupProbe:
            httpGet:
              path: /actuator/health/liveness
              port: 8080
            failureThreshold: 30
            periodSeconds: 10
            # Gives JVM up to 300s to start
          livenessProbe:
            httpGet:
              path: /actuator/health/liveness
              port: 8080
            initialDelaySeconds: 0
            periodSeconds: 10
            failureThreshold: 3
            # Restarts pod after 3 consecutive failures
          readinessProbe:
            httpGet:
              path: /actuator/health/readiness
              port: 8080
            initialDelaySeconds: 0
            periodSeconds: 5
            failureThreshold: 2
            # Removes from Service endpoints after 2 failures
```

```text
Probe decision matrix:
  Startup probe fails  -> keep trying (up to 300s), do NOT restart yet
  Liveness probe fails  -> restart the container (kill and recreate)
  Readiness probe fails -> remove from traffic (keep container alive)
```

### How do gateway and load-balancer health checks provide defense in depth?

The gateway or load balancer periodically checks each backend and removes any that fail from rotation. This is defense in depth: multiple layers each refuse to route to something unhealthy, so even if the registry or Kubernetes has a slight lag, the component actually routing requests independently avoids sending traffic to a bad instance.

```yaml
# Spring Cloud Gateway health check for downstream services
spring:
  cloud:
    gateway:
      discovery:
        locator:
          enabled: true
      routes:
        - id: account-service
          uri: lb://account-service
          predicates:
            - Path=/api/accounts/**
          metadata:
            response-timeout: 2000
            connect-timeout: 500
```

```text
Defense in depth layers:
  Layer 1: Eureka heartbeat      -> evicts dead instance after ~90s
  Layer 2: Kubernetes readiness   -> removes pod from endpoints in ~10s
  Layer 3: Gateway health check   -> stops routing to bad backend in ~5s
  Layer 4: Client-side LB         -> marks instance down after 1 failure
  Result: multiple independent checks catch failures at different speeds
```

### How does monitoring close the loop?

Monitoring turns signals into human action. Metrics like up/down gauges, request error rates, latency percentiles, and probe failures are scraped by Prometheus, visualized in Grafana, and wired to alerts in PagerDuty. Synthetic checks go further by hitting the real API from outside on a schedule, catching problems that internal probes miss.

```java
package com.bank.monitoring;

import io.micrometer.core.instrument.Gauge;
import io.micrometer.core.instrument.MeterRegistry;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class ServiceHealthMetrics {

    public ServiceHealthMetrics(MeterRegistry registry) {
        Gauge.builder("service.health.database", this,
                ServiceHealthMetrics::checkDatabaseHealth)
            .description("1 if database is reachable, 0 otherwise")
            .register(registry);
    }

    private double checkDatabaseHealth() {
        // Returns 1.0 for healthy, 0.0 for unhealthy
        try {
            // Quick DB ping
            return 1.0;
        } catch (Exception e) {
            return 0.0;
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Prometheus alert rules:
  - alert: ServiceDown
    expr: up{job="account-service"} == 0
    for: 1m
    labels:
      severity: critical

  - alert: HighErrorRate
    expr: rate(http_server_requests_seconds_count{status=~"5.."}[5m]) > 0.05
    for: 2m
    labels:
      severity: warning

  - alert: ReadinessDown
    expr: kube_pod_status_ready{condition="false"} == 1
    for: 30s
    labels:
      severity: critical
```

### What happens when readiness is down but liveness is up?

A well-built readiness check verifies real dependencies. If the database pool silently breaks, every request fails while liveness stays green. With separate probes, liveness correctly keeps the process running (restarting would not fix a broken downstream) while readiness reports not-ready and pulls the instance out of rotation.

```text
Scenario: database pool broken
  Liveness:  UP   (JVM process is alive and not stuck)
  Readiness: DOWN (database connection pool broken)

  Kubernetes action: do NOT restart, just remove from Service endpoints
  Result: pod stays alive, no traffic routed to it, recovery possible
         when DB pool recovers -> readiness returns UP -> traffic resumes
```

### What are common mistakes with health checks?

The most common mistake is having a readiness check that only returns a static "UP" without verifying real dependencies. Another mistake is making liveness checks too expensive, which causes them to time out and trigger unnecessary restarts.

```java
package com.bank.health;

import org.springframework.boot.actuate.health.Health;
import org.springframework.boot.actuate.health.HealthIndicator;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

// WRONG: liveness check that does too much
// If the DB is slow, this times out and Kubernetes restarts the pod,
// even though the process is alive. Restarting makes things worse.
@Component
public class WrongLivenessCheck implements HealthIndicator {
    @Override
    public Health health() {
        // Do NOT check external dependencies in liveness!
        // A slow DB query here causes restart loops
        return Health.up().build();
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.health;

import org.springframework.boot.actuate.health.Health;
import org.springframework.boot.actuate.health.HealthIndicator;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

// CORRECT: keep liveness simple, check dependencies only in readiness
@Component
public class CorrectLivenessCheck implements HealthIndicator {
    @Override
    public Health health() {
        // Liveness: am I stuck in a deadlock or infinite loop?
        // Just check thread health, not external dependencies
        if (Thread.currentThread().isInterrupted()) {
            return Health.down().withDetail("reason", "interrupted").build();
        }
        return Health.up().build();
    }
}
```

```text
Wrong: liveness checks DB -> DB slow -> timeout -> pod restart -> worse
Correct: liveness checks JVM health -> readiness checks DB -> pod removed from traffic, not killed
```

### What should a senior engineer know about health hierarchies?

Up is not a single signal but a hierarchy: the process is alive, it is ready, dependencies are healthy, end-to-end requests succeed, and someone is alerted the instant any layer degrades.

| Level | Signal | Tool | Action |
|---|---|---|---|
| Process alive | Liveness probe | Kubernetes | Restart if stuck |
| Ready for traffic | Readiness probe | Kubernetes | Remove from endpoints |
| Dependencies healthy | Custom health indicators | Actuator | Report to dashboards |
| End-to-end success | Synthetic checks | External monitor | Alert on-call |
| Trend degradation | Latency percentiles | Prometheus/Grafana | Investigate proactively |
